Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "PNR failure (suppression trigger)",
    "counter strategy",
    "PULL_UP as counter after PNR failure",
    "lift (counter efficiency gain)",
    "league-wide counter evidence",
    "unit-level consistency threshold (>=70% lift>0, median>2pp)"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define PNR failure event in a way that avoids regression-to-the-mean and look-ahead bias?",
    "What is the appropriate unit of analysis (team, team-season, matchup) given exposure sparsity?",
    "How to measure lift: differencing counter PULL_UP PPP versus baseline PULL_UP PPP in similar contexts without PNR failure?",
    "How to control for confounding from overall defensive strength and game situation?",
    "What is the statistical power to test the dual threshold criteria (>=70% positive, median>2pp) given plausible unit counts?",
    "How reliable are the play-type labels (PNR, PULL_UP) in the derived possession data?",
    "How to avoid selecting counter play type post-hoc and maintain a focused test on PULL_UP?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-063",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Pre-game play-type PPP rankings from training data correlated with actual efficiency in held-out games: median Spearman rho +0.539, 88.4% of rows had positive rho, median lift +0.4261 PPP, 90.8% positive lift. Top-3 lift partly reflects league-wide high-efficiency play types (CUT, PUTBACK, TRANSITION). Caveat: 10.5% of rows the model misled (rho<0). Risk: DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-034",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Across 870 ordered team pairs, an opponent-specific exploitable defensive weakness existed in 33.3% of pairs, traps in 23.3%, both in 6.7%, neither in 50.0%. Median best edge was 3.79%. Edges depended only on the defense (e.g., DAL PNR +9.2% for every offense) and were not matchup-specific."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-066",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "In four playoff games, team-playtype lifts were small (ORL vs DET PNR +1.2pp; POR vs SAS all negative) and player-channel lifts were large but based on tiny sample sizes (n=15-28 defensive possessions). Primary defender attribution included helper steals and no holdout validation was performed."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-058",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "2024-25 shot-level PNR share of half-court shots ranged from OKC 7.567% to IND 13.824%. Rates served as calibration inputs and implied full-possession PNR rates of approximately 17.9-19.6%."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-066",
      "what_failed": "Player-channel matchup decomposition of counter play types at the individual defender-initiator level",
      "why": "Question not answerable with available data due to extreme exposure sparsity (defensive possessions per cell often 15-28), plus attribution problems (helper steals included). Failure mechanism recorded as A_unanswerable, limited by LIM:insufficient_exposure."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "The project's play-type classification can rank offensive PPP with moderate out-of-sample validity (FAM:SF-APPA-063).",
    "Defensive edges on specific play types exist league-wide but are often defense-driven rather than matchup-specific (FAM:SF-APPA-034).",
    "Play-type possession rates are stable enough to calibrate PNR labels (FAM:SF-APPM-058).",
    "Exposure sparsity makes fine-grained (player-level) counter-lift estimates unreliable (FAM:SF-APPA-066)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether PULL_UP specifically functions as an effective league-wide counter after PNR failure.",
    "The magnitude, direction, or prevalence of PULL_UP lift following identified PNR suppression events.",
    "That threshold criteria (>=70% of units with lift>0, median lift>2pp) are plausible or achievable.",
    "Causal attribution of any observed lift to a counter effect rather than regression to the mean, confounding, or defensive quality."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Defining units at the team or team-season level (rather than player channels) can avoid the exposure sparsity that made FAM:SF-APPA-066 untestable.",
    "Any analysis that defines a PNR failure event using low PPP in a window will face substantial regression-to-the-mean bias in the subsequent window unless the baseline is conditioned on the same failure-selection criteria.",
    "The requirement that median lift exceeds 2 points per 100 possessions (0.02 PPP) is a high bar relative to the defensive edge magnitudes seen in FAM:SF-APPA-034 (median edge 3.79% on usage, not efficiency) and typical NBA play-type margins.",
    "Testing whether >=70% of units show positive lift with 30 teams has limited power; pooling across team-seasons (up to ~120 units) may be necessary but requires appropriate handling of within-team correlation.",
    "The hypothesis is specific to PULL_UP as a counter, which reduces multiple-testing concerns but raises the possibility that the choice of PULL_UP was data-driven from prior exploration; pre-registration mitigates this."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact operational definition of 'PNR fail' is not specified in the question and could be implemented in multiple ways (half-game trigger, rolling game window, possession-level condition), each with different bias-variance trade-offs.",
    "Whether the 'unit' is team, team-season, or another grouping is not stated; this choice strongly affects statistical power and the interpretation of 'league-wide'.",
    "The lift metric's denominator and baseline are unspecified; lift could be defined as difference in PPP, relative risk, or absolute effect, and the choice influences the plausibility of the 2pp median threshold.",
    "The temporal scope of the counter: does lift decay over time, and should it be measured in the same game, next game, or over a longer horizon?",
    "Potential for confounders such as opponent defensive strength, game location, rest, and injuries to create spurious lift signals if not controlled."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no",
    "explanation": "No prior study directly addressed the PNR-failure to PULL_UP-counter question, so the decision to proceed is based on current feasibility. However, the historical sparsity finding (FAM:SF-APPA-066) strongly informs the design choice to avoid player-level units, and the validation of play-type rankings (FAM:SF-APPA-063) gives moderate confidence in measurement reliability. The absence of prior evidence on this specific link means the study is novel but must address confounders de novo."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.6,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "If PNR failure is defined by low PPP in a window, subsequent PULL_UP efficiency will regress to the mean, creating spurious lift. A failure definition based on a separate period (e.g., prior games) and a within-unit control period can mitigate this."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "If units are defined below the team level (e.g., lineup, player-pair), the number of PNR failure events followed by PULL_UP counters per unit may be too small for reliable lift estimation, as documented in FAM:SF-APPA-066."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Overall opponent defensive strength and game-state factors (score margin, period, rest) may drive both PNR failure and subsequent PULL_UP outcomes, confounding the counter effect."
      },
      {
        "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
        "text": "Identifying failure and counter within the same game using complete-game data introduces look-ahead bias; a clean separation of the identification window and outcome window is needed."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Both PNR and PULL_UP are derived play-type labels with known inferrer limitations; misclassification could attenuate or inflate lift estimates."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-APPA-063",
      "FAM:SF-APPA-034",
      "FAM:SF-APPA-066",
      "FAM:SF-APPM-058"
    ],
    "summary": "No earlier study tested whether PULL_UP is a league-wide counter after PNR failure. FAM:SF-APPA-063 validated play-type efficiency ranking, FAM:SF-APPA-034 described defensive weaknesses but not counter sequences, FAM:SF-APPA-066 showed player-level decomposition fails due to sparsity, and FAM:SF-APPM-058 documented PNR label rates. The proposed question builds on these foundations but addresses a novel causal chain."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "If PNR failure is defined by a dip in PPP, the subsequent period's PULL_UP performance will naturally regress toward its mean, producing a positive 'lift' that is not a causal counter effect. Requires conditioning on the selection mechanism or using an independent baseline."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Opponent defensive strength, game location, rest days, and lineup composition may simultaneously lower PNR PPP and also affect PULL_UP efficiency, creating a spurious association between failure and counter success."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Using within-game half-to-half triggers (as in the project's existing suppression-trigger concept) can leak future information if the counter outcome is measured in the same half or if the trigger definition relies on the full half's data before the outcome window is properly separated."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Even at the team-season level, the number of qualifying PNR failure events followed by sufficient PULL_UP possessions may be small for some teams, producing noisy unit-level lift estimates that underpower the >=70% threshold test."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Play-type classification errors for PNR (especially ball-handler vs. roll-man) and PULL_UP (often inferred from shot distance and dribble context) can bias lift estimates either towards or away from the null."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Play-type efficiencies and counter dynamics may shift across seasons (2022-23 through 2025-26) due to rule changes, strategic evolution, or roster turnover, violating the stationarity assumption of a pooled analysis."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "With 30 teams, testing that >=70% show positive lift has low statistical power (e.g., ~0.35 for a one-tailed binomial test at alpha=0.05 if the true proportion is 0.7 vs null 0.5). Aggregating team-seasons (~120 units) improves power but introduces non-independence that must be modeled."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Although the hypothesis names only PULL_UP as the counter, if the choice was informed by exploratory analysis of multiple candidate play types, the effective number of tests must be adjusted. Pre-registration eliminates this, but the possibility of undiagnosed data-peeking remains a risk."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.15,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.25,
    "artifact": 0.35,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of an offense choosing PULL_UP possessions (vs. not choosing PULL_UP) following an identified PNR failure event on points per possession, defined as the average difference in PULL_UP PPP in games immediately following a failure event compared to PULL_UP PPP in comparable non-failure games for the same team, adjusting for opponent defensive strength and game context.",
    "unit": "team-season (30 teams × up to 4 seasons, target ~120 units). Primary inference on team-season-level lift; secondary summary checks at the team level (all seasons pooled) to satisfy the league-wide consistency criterion.",
    "sample": "All regular-season and playoff games from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26. Exclude garbage time (Q4/OT with margin >20 pts). A PNR failure event is defined as a game where the team's PNR PPP falls below its season-to-date PNR PPP by >1.5 standard errors (rolling window, min 5 prior games). The counter window is the team's immediately following game (next game, same or different opponent), restricting to PULL_UP possessions within that game. Include only team-seasons with ≥5 failure events and ≥30 total counter PULL_UP possessions.",
    "power": "Assuming 120 team-seasons after exclusions, a one-tailed binomial test of proportion >0.5 with true proportion 0.7 has power ~0.8 at alpha=0.05. For the median lift >2pp, power depends on the variance of unit lift estimates; with 120 units we expect moderate power to detect a median shift of ~3-4 pp/100 from zero, but thresholds near 2pp may remain underpowered. Simulation of the specific design is recommended before analysis.",
    "controls": "In each unit-level regression (or mixed-effects model): opponent defensive rating for the game, opponent's season PNR PPP allowed, game location (home/away), rest advantage, and the team's own season PULL_UP PPP in non-failure games (to isolate failure-related lift). Team-season random intercepts account for unit heterogeneity.",
    "falsification": "1) Run the same analysis using a placebo play type (e.g., CUT) that is not hypothesized as a PNR counter; lift should not exceed the same thresholds. 2) Permute failure labels within team-season (shuffling which games are labeled 'failure') and recompute lift; the distribution of permuted lifts should center near zero and rarely meet the >=70% and median>2pp criteria. 3) Test within the same game using H2 counter after H1 failure (if using a separate holdout season) to check if results are consistent despite different temporal resolution.",
    "multiplicity": "Only one counter play type (PULL_UP) is tested, so no family-wise correction is required. The dual-threshold decision rule (proportion >0, median >2pp) is treated as a conjunction; both must be met. No interim peeking.",
    "decision_rule": "Hypothesis is supported if, across all eligible team-season units, (a) the proportion of units with lift > 0 is >=0.70 and a one-tailed exact binomial test rejects the null proportion 0.50 at alpha=0.05; AND (b) the median unit lift exceeds 0.02 PPP (2 points per 100 possessions) with a one-sided bootstrap percentile confidence interval (10,000 resamples) whose lower bound excludes 0.02 PPP. Both conditions must be satisfied. Results meeting only one condition are considered inconclusive.",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API play-by-play and derived possession files frozen as of 2026-06-21 (playoffs concluded). The analysis will use the project's existing play-type classifier (inferrer) without post-hoc label adjustments. Any updates to the inferrer after the data freeze date must be treated as a separate sensitivity analysis."
  }
}}

